docs/scratch/arrival-branch-review/codex-r3-stderr.log:5365:01M070N08HX69QAEQ3TRPY72CQ	decision	design/declared-fold-order	AMENDMENT 2026-08-16 #4 — RATIFIED (Kyle, in-session with Opus 5, cross-family with GPT 5.6 "Sol", grounded by a 5-lens code audit, workflow wf_5c2a2768-b76, 22 agents). THIS SUPERSEDES THE TWO-MODE DESIGN ENTIRELY, including amendments #1 and #3 of this decision and load-bearing sentences of decision:design/log-and-index-roles. The store is no longer a thing with modes.\n\n=== THE COLLAPSE ===\n\nTHE STORE IS AN APPEND-ONLY, LINEAGE-LOCAL ARRIVAL LOG. It holds complete canonical records plus a minted arrival lineage genesis. Its position is the witness position. It is never merged, never rebuilt, never rewound. EVERYTHING ELSE IS A PROJECTION OF AN ARRIVAL PREFIX: fold state, FTS/search, graph views, lenses, the sqlite index, AND the git-tracked JSONL, which becomes a deterministic canonical-set synchronization projection. Authority INVERTS from today, where JSONL is canonical and custody order is implicit in line order.\n\nOne sentence: THE STORE IS WHAT THIS CUSTODIAN RECEIVED. Everything else is a question asked of it.\n\n(thing, id) AS A SUBSTRATE PRIMITIVE — Kyle's generalization, which is what finished the collapse. "Event order" was only ever (ts, id), with ts the first and most obvious key; fiction_time, effective_date, priority, (chapter, scene) are peers. So "event" leaves the vocabulary and the substrate exposes ONE read primitive:\n    ordered(prefix=W, key=K) = stable total ordering of arrival[0:W] by (K(record), record.id)\nwith `order` declaring `arrival` or `by KEY`. Requirements on K: deterministic under its declaration generation, historically reconstructible, replica-independent, totally comparable. That is sufficient to make it substrate-level WITHOUT teaching the substrate what ts or fiction_time mean — domain meaning stays in declarations. id remains identity/dedup plus deterministic totalization and is NEVER semantic time; protecting that is more important now than before, because most keys are not time at all.\n\nPROJECTION TAXONOMY — the real descendant of the trilemma, which was never a property of stores. It classifies any maintained projection, not just folds:\n    arrival-stable          new observations only extend it -> resume from head\n    key-ordered/insertion-sensitive   a new observation may sort into prior material -> checkpoint + replay from insertion\n    order-independent       visitation order does not matter -> incorporate directly\nThe checkpoint ladder is therefore projection-maintenance infrastructure, not an event-store feature. FTS is the clarifying case: it exists today partly because sqlite happens to provide FTS5 — an accidental capability of the storage engine promoted to a capability of the system. The .vertex `search` directive is already a projection spec, mislabeled.\n\nWITNESS POSITION = (arrival_lineage, ordinal). NOT (replica, position) — the naming matters and Sol's correction is adopted. "Replica" names machine/worktree/runtime identity; what makes a persisted position meaningful is the MINTED GENESIS LINEAGE of the arrival log. Renaming or moving a worktree must not change a position's identity, and copying bytes must not silently establish continuity with a lineage — which is exactly the hijack AmbiguousGenesis already refuses. The type states the law: POSITIONS COMPARE ONLY WITHIN ONE ARRIVAL LINEAGE, and cross-lineage comparison is not false but UNDEFINED, failing structurally rather than answering wrongly. One coordinate carries CAS token, tick chain basis and witness cursor simultaneously; they must not be three notions of head.\n\nCAS IS A QUALIFIED PREDICATE OVER THE ARRIVAL AXIS, not a second species. Contract: "no fact matching the declaration-basis predicate arrived in lineage L after position P" — preserving today's lineage-filtered, _decl.*-only semantics (sqlite_store.py:1411-1438) while riding the new axis. An UNQUALIFIED arrival-head compare was verified to break ordinary use: any `sl emit` landing in the plan->apply window would raise StaleDeclarationHead across six call sites (libs/sdk/src/sdk/kind.py:148/152,194/198,239/243,314/318,361/365; apps/loops/src/loops/commands/store.py:1257->1356) with an error message that would have become false. Digest CAS stays RETIRED — demoted to a possible conflict-narrowing optimization, not protocol.\n\nFEDERATED READ AND ADMISSION STAY DISTINCT, and the audit confirms the code already holds this line — the rewrite does NOT need to absorb aggregation.\n    federated read: inspect evidence resident in independent stores; witness axes remain separate. combine/discover use read-only ATTACH, copy nothing (vertex_reader.py:9-11, :335-341), and REFUSE to fabricate a global witness order across stores (WitnessAggregateUnsupported, plus the A10 cross-store lineage refusal).\n    admission: this lineage receives a foreign observation; canonical bytes append to this arrival log; this lineage now holds a receipt position for it. A DISTINCT CUSTODY ACT, separate from merge. Signature verification at admission is NEW CODE — no current receive path has it.\nProtect this distinction in the new store API.\n\nTHE SHARED PROJECTION AND GIT. Git-tracking the store IS the intended workflow (Kyle, confirmed) and has been the intent since the JSONL work began; .gitignore:9 (*.jsonl) and :37 (.loops/) are RESIDUE to sweep in the same change that lands arrival, at which point .loops/project.vertex:5-7's claim becomes true instead of aspirational. CORRECTION TO A CLAIM MADE IN-SESSION AND WITHDRAWN: materializing the projection in canonical id order does NOT mostly eliminate git tail conflicts. ULIDs are time-ordered, so records newly emitted on two branches both sort after their common base — both branches still change EOF from the same base, which is the classic append-conflict shape. WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the part that matters: a given fact set has EXACTLY ONE canonical byte representation, giving deterministic projection generation, cheap digest equality, and the removal of representation order as semantic information. THE RIGHT ANSWER for merge is available precisely because the projection is derived: give git a SEMANTIC MERGE DRIVER (parse ours/base/theirs, union by id, conflict on same-id-different-bytes, validate policy, sort canonically, rewrite). The audit found NO .gitattributes and no merge driver anywhere in the tree, so this is greenfield, not a compatibility constraint. Loops defines the semantics of merging its own representation; git remains the PR/concurrency workflow.\n\n=== WHY THIS IS NOT MERELY AESTHETIC: REPO EVIDENCE ===\n\nThe audit did not just agree with the collapse; it found a CORRECTNESS WEAKNESS exactly where the new model says authority is misplaced.\n\nTHE AUTHORITY TEST, ANSWERED LITERALLY: delete project.jsonl today with a complete valid arrival log present and NOTHING can be answered — not one local semantic or integrity claim. catch_up refuses to open (JsonlCanonicalUnsupported), audit_agreement fails, preflight gates stats/ticks/verify (preflight.py:67-72).\n\nLATENT DEFECT ON MAIN: line order IS custody order and is CRYPTOGRAPHICALLY COMMITTED TO — prev_hash, window_start, fact_cursor and window_hash all derive from rowid (sqlite_store.py:1595-1603, window SQL :1531-1534; declared authority at :85-88 and :12-13). But _rebuild does DELETE FROM facts, resetting sqlite's rowid counter, and the code says it plainly (jsonl_store.py:931-934): "re-indexed rows take rowids that previously named other facts." CHAIN COMMITMENTS REST ON AN AXIS A REBUILD REGENERATES. Separately, byte offset is the custody watermark and is NOT confined to --deep: the default L1 gate is a byte-prefix model (_check_offset canonical_audit.py:429-460, _suffix_unindexed :400-427, _check_last_line :495-540), so a mid-file insertion invalidates it with no deep verification run.\n\nTHE DESIGN ANTICIPATED THIS. engine/witness.py:25-30: "A store-wide receipt ordinal (GlobalReceiptPosition) is a queued protocol amendment, not smuggled in here." ARRIVAL IS EXACTLY THAT QUEUED AMENDMENT; witness.py:3-4 and :22-24 already carry the inclusive-witness-prefix vocabulary.\n\n=== PR #8: MERGE, DO NOT RELEASE, DO NOT LEAVE IT ALONE ON MAIN ===\n\nFar less superseded than assumed. It introduces NO per-store receipt-mode flag: it unconditionally makes fold order = the order this store received facts and demotes (ts,id) to an explicit read lens. That IS the arrival model's semantics, implemented one layer too low — its authority for "order received" is rowid, which today equals line order. Not merging costs more: the rewrite would have to re-fight every (ts,id)-as-fold-order pin still on main (conformance replay vectors, test_properties_replay.py, test_fold_diff.py, libs/store/tests/test_merge.py:452) and rebuild replay_from plus checkpoint dispatch from zero with no base-arm benchmark evidence.\n    KEEP: Spec.replay_from (atoms/spec.py:128 — arrival order IS append order, so only a docstring word moves); the VertexHandle checkpoint machinery (handle.py:792, :1225) with same-snapshot seeding, epoch invalidation and StoreReplaced-over-reinterpretation already solved; the benchmark arms (engine_receive_held -69.2%/-86.5%, same-runner A/B/A/B/A) with their honest self-stated boundaries; the lens conformance area (spec/conformance/generate_lens.py — hardcoded to ts, i.e. ONE INSTANTIATION SHORT of the (key,id) generalization, so extend rather than rebuild); Rule 17's prose ratchet, which holds verbatim under arrival.\n    REWRITE: the CAS token coordinate ((rowid,id) -> arrival head) — LARGER than the branch notes, since the token escapes into a PERSISTED intent record (ceremony.py:432); the combined-read fold fork (vertex_reader.py:390,:403-406 and libs/sdk/src/sdk/read.py), which the target answers directly — a combined read has no single arrival axis, so it must DECLARE a projection ordering and the member-count fork disappears; authority prose across store_reader.py, witness.py, declaration.py, docs.\n    DISCARD: R1 as doctrine only (merge.py:83 "the ceremony DEFINES the merged store's receipt order" and test_merge_direction_sets_fold_order_by_receipt). Under the target, merge never touches custody. Keep test_merge_direction_is_deterministic.\n    INTERIM HAZARD, operationally binding: merged alone, fold results depend on git's line interleaving of the still-canonical JSONL and the branch adds no guard; detection exists at verify time only. So PR #8 MUST NOT SPEND MEANINGFUL TIME ON MAIN BY ITSELF — land it with arrival, or accept that multi-replica JSONL merges are fold-affecting in the interim. No tag, no release, no user-visible intermediate store contract; the one contract break is spent on the model actually believed.\n\n=== THE FOUR CUTS (replacing the dead 8-slice plan) ===\n\nA AUTHORITY — arrival becomes canonical; lineage genesis moves into it; records become complete. residence.py:56-62 is the whole mode switch (a suffix test), ~225 non-test call sites funnel through it. Sweep hardcoded .db sites, especially init.py:141-142 whose regex SILENTLY NO-OPS on a non-.db locator. Reuse _read_lines/_truncate_torn_line, which migrate to arrival WHERE THEY ARE CORRECT since arrival is the genuinely append-only artifact. own_lineage is written by exactly two ceremonies (absorb_genesis sqlite_store.py:932-935, adopt_lineage :1392-1395).\nB PROJECTIONS — catch_up/_rebuild are already the right primitive; re-point the source and add .jsonl as a SECOND derived output. The offset/count triple moves to arrival; the .jsonl side re-keys to SET MEMBERSHIP, since a mergeable projection has no stable byte offset. merge_store/receive_store rewritten to append into arrival and let both projections re-derive — never direct index INSERTs. reanchor (jsonl_store.py:152-156) becomes a PERMANENT REFUSAL: arrival is never rewound, which retires the queued log-rewrite ceremony item.\nC ORDERING — declared projection order; arrival | by KEY; id as totalizer. _receipt_order (libs/store/src/store/jsonl.py:104-126) DISSOLVES — it exists only because there is no durable receipt ordinal, and arrival supplies one. Extend generate_lens.py from ts-only to arbitrary declared keys. CAS per above.\nD SURFACES — canonical_audit.py is the one place that FIGHTS BACK: respecifying only the deep check is insufficient, because the L1 default gate is order-dependent too; the whole offset/prefix custody model moves to arrival and the jsonl comparison becomes set-membership. Blast radius contained to one call site. preflight.py survives UNTOUCHED — its pure-reader contract ("repair destroys the evidence verification exists to inspect") and the Check.beyond_offset scope-not-verdict discipline are the cleanest things in the audited surface; only the artifact pair changes. Seals re-base on arrival position. Aggregation keeps WitnessAggregateUnsupported and A10 verbatim.\n\n=== THE ONE MIGRATION — WHERE THE REMAINING SKEPTICISM GOES ===\n\nTemplate is `rebirth` (fully receipted, precedent fact 01KTY5RNA96DSC1FW9R7NC69RT with source hashes and counts, sealed by a genesis tick), NOT the JSONL flip, which left NO receipt in the fact stream at all and whose only trace is store_meta plus a manual .pre-jsonl snapshot — a convention appearing nowhere in code.\n\nRATIFIED CONSTRAINT — DO NOT MANUFACTURE HISTORICAL PRECISION. _receipt_order carries a documented LOSSY heuristic for ticks with null or unresolvable fact_cursor (jsonl.py:35-40), and a heuristic written into an append-only arrival log is PERMANENT epistemic history. Where a legacy store does not contain enough information to reconstruct exact historical receipt order, the migration MUST mint an explicit legacy-adoption genesis marker — basis = imported legacy set, historical receipt UNAVAILABLE before genesis — with exact receipt semantics from that boundary onward. A named incomplete state over a convenient lie, which is the conceptual model's own stance. The migration spec must ESTABLISH which stores permit exact migration rather than letting the heuristic silently become history. THIS IS THE THING TO ADVERSARIALLY TEST NEXT.\nPopulation: locally ONE jsonl-canonical store (project.jsonl, 156MB, 3720 facts/92 ticks) where arrival is a LOSSLESS re-encode of existing line order, and six sqlite-canonical config vertices whose fact/tick interleave must go through _receipt_order and therefore inherit the heuristic.\nIDEMPOTENCE AND RESUMABILITY ARE RELEASE BLOCKERS, not nice-to-haves: every migration writer today refuses a pre-existing target, so an interrupted run on 150MB+ artifacts restarts from zero and errors on retry; rebirth assembles across two connections and two commits, and a crash between them leaves a receipt with no sealing tick that the never-overwrite guard makes un-retryable — annoying for rebirth, UNRECOVERABLE for an append-only arrival. Arrival is append-only, so resume-from-offset is natural: DESIGN IT IN. Carry both non-determinism carve-outs (receipt and genesis tick) explicitly, or derive them deterministically from source content.\n\n=== WHAT THIS DELETES FROM THE RECORD ===\nunion/ceremony discipline; the mergeability rollup and its construction ratchet; the S3 asymmetric override (no substrate asymmetry remains — a receipt fold is witness-relative BY DEFINITION, not a stronger ask); two CAS species; two merge legalities; "attestation grade is part of the store type" (custody substrate is uniform, so custody attestation is uniformly AVAILABLE); the positions law (now trivially true); the D7 rewind/residence question (a checkout dropping admitted facts is a DIVERGENT PROJECTION repaired by re-projecting from arrival — nothing rewinds, and FACTS DO NOT BELONG TO BRANCHES); and the trilemma as a store-level choice.\nSURVIVES: the three-clock ontology; receipt stamps, now universal; minted identity; admission as a distinct custody act; witness-relative derivation publishing TESTIMONY ABOUT A DERIVATION (a tick remains a published conclusion reached through a fold; portability does not retroactively make its production a raw observation); declaration-generation pinning in ticks. TICK = conclusion + declaration generation + witness basis + provenance/attestation.\n\n=== OPEN, CARRIED FORWARD ===\nUnknowns the audit named and could not settle: whether promoting own_lineage into arrival collides with existing _decl.genesis lineage semantics; WHETHER A REBUILD INVALIDATES OUTSTANDING WITNESS POSITIONS (rowids are reassigned; nobody traced whether a previously-issued seq: handle silently names a different fact after rebuild — the natural next question); the loops-go conformance oracle and siftd are NOT IN THIS TREE, so a mirrored CAS or ordering implementation there is unaudited (flagged independently by three of five audits, the most consistently raised blind spot); rebirth's source_content_sha256 witness-order derivation; libs/sign and the observer-key registry untraced for other bindings to file position; the arrival suffix name is undecided and unchecked for collision; the 216 ticks that re-entered as tick.* facts in the 2026-06-12 rebirth need a semantic ruling.\nMETHODOLOGICAL CAVEATS: no audit ran tests, so test-coverage claims come from diff text; two-process concurrency against one jsonl-canonical store was never exercised empirically; NO GIT MERGE OF A JSONL LOG WAS EVER PERFORMED; and one verification pass ran against main instead of the branch worktree and produced a FALSE REFUTED, corrected in synthesis — branch-scoped citations require the worktree at /Users/kaygee/Code/loops-wt/receipt-order-fold.\n\nDISPOSITION: architecture RATIFIED and closed to redesign absent implementation evidence contradicting an invariant. Next work is ratification residue (propagate into log-and-index-roles, rewrite memo and plan FROM this fact) and implementation proof, with migration exactness as the first adversarial target.
