   255	`test_every_arrival_named_engine_module_is_scanned` globs
   256	`libs/engine/src/engine/arrival*.py` and fails if a new one is absent from `_SCAN_TARGETS`
   257	(`test_rule_18_arrival_vocabulary_denylist.py:393-407`). Naming the module `arrival_*` enrolls
   258	it in the vocabulary ratchet **by birth**; naming it `head_attestation.py` would require a
   259	manual `_SCAN_TARGETS` edit that a future reader can forget. Ratchet test: prefer the name
   260	that makes the join structural. No `_DENIED` entry is needed — nothing here names retired
   261	vocabulary — and no glossary amendment either, because "head attestation" was already ratified
   262	into the glossary by dispositions §3.
   263	
   264	**Vocabulary hazard, stated so the impl agent does not walk into it.** "Attestation" is already
   265	load-bearing in this codebase for a *different* concept: `FactAttestation`/`TickAttestation`
   266	are the signed receipt on a committed row (`vertex.py:135-136`), and "genesis is the lineage's
   267	attestation root" recurs across `arrival.py:885`, `sqlite_store.py:963-964`, `ceremony.py:517`.
   268	The disposition ruled our vocabulary anyway, and it is right — but "distinct types, distinct
   269	serialization names" (`plan.md:52`) is doing real work here, not just separating us from
   270	`witness.py`. Hence the `type` string in B.3 is `arrival-head-observation`, not the doc's
   271	signed `arrival-head`, which also leaves the future signed corpus unencumbered.
   272	
   273	### B.2 The types
   274	
   275	```python
   276	class Kind(Enum):        # why this entry exists
   277	    BOOTSTRAP   = "bootstrap"    # first entry for a lineage
   278	    ADVANCE     = "advance"      # the head moved
   279	    AUDIT       = "audit"        # a full chain-plus-projection audit ran
   280	    TRUST_RESET = "trust-reset"  # an operator ceremony re-established trust
   281	
   282	class Level(Enum):       # what evidence backed this entry's head
   283	    MINT          = "mint"           # this process minted the genesis
   284	    FIRST_CONTACT = "first-contact"  # trust on first use; nothing was proven
   285	    COMMIT        = "commit"         # this process appended it under the full-head fence
   286	    DESCENDANT    = "descendant"     # a verified suffix walk from the previous head
   287	    FULL          = "full"           # verified from genesis
   288	
   289	@dataclass(frozen=True)
   290	class HeadAttestation:
   291	    head: Head                 # lineage + ordinal + record_hash — the comparison keys
   292	    kind: Kind
   293	    level: Level
   294	    observed_at: float
   295	    location: str = ""         # where it was seen; diagnosis only, never a key
   296	    note: str = ""             # trust-reset gap description, audit result
   297	```
   298	
   299	Field choices, against the §04 table (`witness-protocol.html:210-233`):
   300	
   301	- **The whole `Head`, always** — §04's `lineage`/`ordinal`/`record_hash` are exactly
   302	  `arrival_contract.Head`, and reusing the ratified type rather than restating three fields
   303	  means the comparison cannot drift from the CAS pin's notion of a head
   304	  (`arrival_contract.py:120-133`).
   305	- **Counts omitted.** Dispositions §2: they are projection corroboration smuggled into a
   306	  custody claim. Not optional-and-unset — absent from the type.
   307	- **`sig`/`issuer`/`key_id`/`previous` absent.** Deferred grammar (dispositions §1). Their
   308	  absence is the honest shape of an unsigned local cache; adding empty fields would advertise
   309	  a claim this slice does not make.
   310	- **`protocol`/`wire` omitted from the record**, carried by the file header instead (B.3) —
   311	  they are properties of the journal, not of each entry, and re-stating them per line is a
   312	  field nothing reads. Same discipline the registry applied to `lineage`/`role`
   313	  (`arrival_registry.py:94-97`): parsing fields nothing consumes is how speculative grammar
   314	  ossifies.
   315	- **`observed_at` kept.** It is what makes a refusal actionable ("your cached head is from
   316	  three weeks ago") and what answers audit staleness at open. §04 is explicit that it is
   317	  witness metadata and never ledger order.
   318	- **`level` is the scope-the-claim field** and it is the one addition to §04's table. It states
   319	  how much was actually established, so an entry can never be read as a stronger claim than
   320	  the evidence behind it. §12's checkpoint discussion asks for exactly this
   321	  (`witness-protocol.html:583-589`: a checkpoint retains "completion status").
   322	
   323	The **bootstrap receipt** is `Kind.BOOTSTRAP` — with `Level.MINT` when slice 4's sidecar minted
   324	the genesis in this process, and `Level.FIRST_CONTACT` when an already-existing store was
   325	opened for the first time. **Slice 6's verification depends on that distinction**: a journal
   326	beginning at `mint` was witnessed from genesis forward and every head in it is accounted for;
   327	one beginning at `first-contact` was trusted on sight. Slice 4 must produce the former for
   328	every migrated store, and slice 6 checks it.
   329	
   330	The **periodic full-audit entry** is `Kind.AUDIT` / `Level.FULL`, carrying the head the audit
   331	covered and, in `note`, the projection-agreement result.
   332	
   333	### B.3 Serialization
   334	
   335	One JSON object per line, appended with `O_APPEND`; a single line is one write. The first line
   336	of a new journal is a header stating what the file is:
   337	
   338	```json
   339	{"v":1,"type":"arrival-head-observation","protocol":1,"wire":1}
   340	{"v":1,"kind":"bootstrap","level":"mint","lineage":"01ARZ…","ordinal":0,
   341	 "record_hash":"<64-hex>","observed_at":1787779020.0,"location":"/…/project.arrival"}
   342	```
   343	
   344	- **JSONL, not the wire codec.** The wire-v1 body grammar constrains *arrival records*; a head
   345	  attestation is not one and must not be written by the record codec. Deliberate: the journal
   346	  must remain readable when the store it witnesses cannot be opened at all, which is the
   347	  situation it exists for.
   348	- **`protocol`/`wire` in the header** so a journal written under wire v1 is not silently
   349	  compared against a v2 head whose hashes derive differently.
   350	- **These outlive process restarts and outlive the store**, so the grammar is versioned (`v`)
   351	  and unknown fields are preserved-and-ignored on read rather than refused — a journal written
   352	  by a later build must not make an older build refuse to compare.
   353	
   354	**Three read rules, all load-bearing:**
   355	
   356	0. **A `trust-reset` entry opens a new trust epoch; the read scope is the entries after the
   357	   last one.** Without this the ceremony can never take effect: after an operator accepts a
   358	   restore from ordinal 100 back to 90, the abandoned entries at 91–100 still hold the maximum
   359	   ordinal, *K* reads as the head that was deliberately abandoned, and every subsequent open
   360	   refuses `ROLLBACK` forever. Epoch-scoping applies to all three of: which entry is *K*, the
   361	   equivocation check below, and the audit's all-journaled-heads check (C.4). Pre-reset entries
   362	   are retained **as evidence, not as claims** — that is the whole point of recording the gap.
   363	
   364	1. **The known head *K* is the entry with the maximum ordinal, not the last line.** Two writers
   365	   append concurrently (the arrival flock serializes their *appends*, not their journal
   366	   writes), so lines can land out of order: A commits ordinal 5, B commits 6 and journals it, A
   367	   journals 5 last. Last-line semantics would then read K=5 and classify a genuine rollback to
   368	   5 as UNCHANGED. Max-ordinal closes it, and duplicate entries at one ordinal are harmless.
   369	2. **Two entries at the maximum ordinal with different `record_hash` is a typed refusal** —
   370	   journal-internal equivocation. The journal has recorded two incompatible histories and it is
   371	   not the journal's place to choose.
   372	
   373	A torn last line is tolerated on read (the entry is skipped) and reported. It costs at most the
   374	newest observation, which is the same exposure §02's detection window already names
   375	(`witness-protocol.html:150-157`).
   376	
   377	---
   378	
   379	## C. The comparison state machine
   380	
   381	### C.1 Six outcomes as one pure function
   382	
   383	```python
   384	def compare(known: HeadAttestation | None,
   385	            presented: Head,
   386	            at_known: Head | None) -> Outcome
   387	```
   388	
   389	Pure, stdlib-only, no I/O — which is what makes it directly vector-testable and what keeps the
   390	vectors free of any implementation's exception family. `at_known` is the head the ledger
     1	"""The witness minimum — the record, the journal, and the comparison.
     2	
     3	Slice 3 / WP1 (``design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum`` §§A-C).
     4	
     5	Every test here runs against a temporary state root. The module resolves its
     6	home from ``$XDG_STATE_HOME`` at call time and the fixture below redirects it
     7	for the whole module, so no test can reach the developer's real journals — a
     8	suite that wrote head observations into ``~/.local/state/loops`` would be
     9	corrupting the very memory the design exists to protect.
    10	"""
    11	
    12	from __future__ import annotations
    13	
    14	import json
    15	import os
    16	import subprocess
    17	import sys
    18	from pathlib import Path
    19	
    20	import pytest
