4236:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17T5EGH8K3ZJCWK3MFMV3XB","kind":"finding","ts":1788042459.501435,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-mid-file-journal-damage-unstated\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-impl\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"implementer\", \"message\": \"S3WP1-2 (reportable, non-blocking, DECIDED IN BUILD at 0e6421fa) \\u2014 \\u00a7B.3 states the torn-tail rule only for the LAST line ('a torn last line is tolerated on read and reported'), and says nothing about a line that fails to parse EARLIER in the file. The two cases are not the same: a torn tail is what a crash mid-append looks like and costs the newest observation, whereas mid-file damage is not an append artifact at all and skipping it would silently LOWER the remembered head \\u2014 the direction an attacker wants this cache moved (skip the entry that records ordinal 100, and a restore to 90 classifies as an advance). Implemented conservatively: torn FINAL line tolerated and reported on JournalRead.skipped; any earlier parse failure raises JournalUnreadable naming the line number. Same reasoning applied to bindings.jsonl in the OPPOSITE direction \\u2014 a malformed binding line is skipped, because a missing binding degrades to first contact, which the binding only ever improved on and never guaranteed. WP2 ACTION: worth one vector each if the family covers journal reads; ARBITER: confirm the conservative arm, since the design left it open.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}
4237:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17TJQGWVVH3HZCKC9N1WQ8N","kind":"finding","ts":1788042894.089742,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-mid-file-journal-damage-unstated\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-impl\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"implementer\", \"message\": \"S3WP1-2 SHARPENED at 444f6b3b \\u2014 the fact the arbiter needs that the first entry did not have: THE MID-FILE CASE IS REACHABLE FROM AN ORDINARY CRASH, not only from tampering. Sequence: a writer crashes mid-append leaving a fragment with no trailing newline; the next commit appends onto it; before the newline guard those two GLUED into one unreadable line (the new entry's bytes lost inside it), and with the guard the fragment survives as its own line \\u2014 but once anything follows it, it is mid-file, so under the conservative policy every subsequent open raises JournalUnreadable FOREVER. So the choice is not 'tolerate tampering vs refuse tampering'; it is whether a routine crash plus one commit escalates to a permanent operator incident requiring manual journal surgery. Pinned as today's honest behavior by test_a_torn_tail_does_not_glue_itself_to_the_next_entry, which asserts the refusal rather than presuming a policy the arbiter has not ruled. ARBITER OPTIONS: (a) keep conservative refusal and give slice 5's CLI a repair ceremony; (b) tolerate a single unparseable line ANYWHERE and report it, accepting that a skipped entry can lower K; (c) tolerate only a line that is a PREFIX of a well-formed entry (torn-append shaped), refusing arbitrary garbage \\u2014 narrowest of the three and the only one that distinguishes the crash from the tamper. Not changing the policy unilaterally.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}
4239:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17TPJRDCE2KP24BMEPR04ZV","kind":"finding","ts":1788043020.25708,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-mid-file-journal-damage-unstated\", \"status\": \"open\", \"agent\": \"arbiter\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"arbiter\", \"message\": \"ARBITER RULING on the mid-file journal-damage question (the impl correctly did not decide unilaterally): TOLERATE AND REPORT, same posture as the torn tail \\u2014 skip the unparseable mid-file line, surface it on JournalRead.skipped, never silently. Grounds: (1) scope-the-claim \\u2014 skip+report is a LOCATION claim ('this line is unreadable, here'); permanent refusal is a verdict ('this journal is compromised') and the prefix-of-well-formed heuristic is an innocence verdict \\u2014 detectors assert locations, the audit level (operator-run) holds escalation judgment; (2) K-safety \\u2014 within a trust epoch journaled ordinals ascend (commits ascend, resets open a NEW epoch), so a mid-file skip cannot lower K whenever a later well-formed entry survives, making it STRICTLY safer than the already-tolerated torn tail (which does cost its entry's ordinal); (3) operational \\u2014 refusing every future open of a lineage over an ordinary-crash artifact converts crash residue into a permanent trust-reset ceremony. CONDITION the impl must verify in code before applying: the ascending-within-epoch invariant actually holds for every journaled kind (bootstrap/advance/audit/trust-reset-opens-new-epoch) \\u2014 if any kind can journal a lower ordinal within an epoch, STOP and report back instead of applying. Structural damage that breaks the read itself (unparseable header) still refuses JournalUnreadable.\"}"}
4241:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17TWCK28T11QACVX53SY56H","kind":"finding","ts":1788043210.545353,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-mid-file-journal-damage-unstated\", \"status\": \"fixed\", \"agent\": \"arbiter\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"arbiter\", \"message\": \"RULING AMENDED after the impl's conditional-stop verification REFUTED the arbiter's mechanism (finding:s3wp1-epoch-ordinals-do-not-ascend-in-file-order \\u2014 journal writes do not ascend in file order even though commits do; empirically: epoch file order [90-reset, 92, 91], mid-file damage to 92 with 91 surviving after it -> tolerated K=91 -> a genuine restore-from-92 presenting 91 classifies UNCHANGED. Silent re-acceptance, the exact failure the max-ordinal rule closes; the arbiter's strictly-safer-than-torn-tail claim was WRONG \\u2014 the mid-file loss can be the epoch max with later lines masking it). AMENDED RULING = the impl's option (c), which keeps the policy and fixes the mechanism: tolerate and report exactly as ruled (skip, surface on .skipped, never silent, never refused; JournalUnreadable scopes to structural damage; escalation lives at the audit level), AND the weakened claim becomes STRUCTURAL \\u2014 when skipped is non-empty the read's K is a LOWER BOUND, not a head: sound for ROLLBACK (presented < bound is definitely rollback), structurally UNABLE to answer UNCHANGED (presented == bound may be rollback from the lost line), so WP3's seam gathers evidence on incomplete reads instead of the O(1) shortcut. Free in the dominant case (.skipped empty on healthy journals). Implementation requirement: the lower-bound state must be UNIGNORABLE BY CONSTRUCTION in the read type (not a boolean beside a plain K a caller can skip) \\u2014 the ratchet standard the impl itself invoked to reject the caller-vigilance option. Options (a) caller vigilance and (b) widening compare's pinned signature REJECTED as the impl argued. RETRO NOTE: the conditional-stop protocol worked \\u2014 the arbiter's ruling carried its own falsification condition and the impl executed it instead of the ruling.\"}"}
4242:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17VCN80Q9KXMRY182JXFZ93","kind":"finding","ts":1788043743.716426,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-mid-file-journal-damage-unstated\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-impl\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"implementer\", \"message\": \"S3WP1-2 CLOSED \\u2014 amended ruling APPLIED at 66df1e71 (branch slice3/arrival-witness, tip be8b6c5e). TOLERATE AND REPORT as ruled: every unreadable line is skipped and surfaced on JournalRead.skipped wherever it sits, never silently and never refused. The K-safety hole the refutation found (finding:s3wp1-epoch-ordinals-do-not-ascend-in-file-order) is closed in the RESULT TYPE rather than by a verdict: JournalRead.known is now EstablishedHead | HeadLowerBound | None, the weakened case has NO entry attribute (its field is at_least), and established_head() raises IndeterminateComparison instead of answering \\u2014 so the cheap 'presented equals K therefore unchanged' shortcut is UNOBTAINABLE against an incomplete journal, not merely discouraged. compare's signature is untouched; no WP2 vector shape changes. TWO CORRECTIONS to my own option (c) as originally worded, both now in the type's docstring: (1) 'the seam gathers evidence instead' is WRONG AS A REPAIR \\u2014 a walk verifies the STORE's chain, but the missing fact is about what this machine previously ACCEPTED, which the store never knew; only journal repair or the trust-reset ceremony resolves it, both operator work. (2) An unreadable line carries NO information about the ordinal it held, so nothing bounds K from above \\u2014 ADVANCED is unsound against a bound too, not only UNCHANGED. WHAT DISSOLVED: the positional torn-tail-vs-mid-file distinction is gone (position was a proxy for 'crash artifact' and a bad one, since a fragment becomes mid-file the moment one commit follows it), and choice 6 with it. JournalUnreadable SURVIVES rescoped to structural damage that breaks the read itself \\u2014 entries with no header, whose protocol/wire hash derivation is then unknown; append now supplies the header when it finds an empty file so a creator that died after winning the exclusive create cannot strand a journal headerless. Counts 2063 -> 2150 (+87), architecture 99 -> 99. Mutation demos: restore the refusal = 7 failed; collapse the bound into a plain head = 8 failed including the cannot-claim-unchanged test.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}
4244:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17WCVD04KNMSCYRS91MM9HN","kind":"finding","ts":1788044798.881554,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-gate-all-entries-unreadable-silent-tofu\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-gate\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"gate\", \"message\": \"S3WP1-GATE-2 (BLOCKING) \\u2014 the amended ruling's unignorable-by-construction property is absent in the zero-surviving-entry corner. If every entry line is unreadable, _known_of gets an empty epoch, `known` stays None, and the HeadLowerBound branch is never taken: established_head() returns None WITHOUT raising, compare answers FIRST_CONTACT, and the losses sit only in .skipped. That is exactly the shape the ruling forbade \\u2014 \\\"not a boolean beside a plain K a caller can skip\\\". The tolerate-and-report half is satisfied; the unignorable half is not. VERIFIED with no attacker, only version skew: a journal written entirely by a later build (v2 header + v2 entries) reads as entries=0, skipped=3, known=None, bootstrap=None, and a FRESH GENESIS at ordinal 0 presented against a journal remembering ordinal 4217 classifies FIRST-CONTACT. That is the design's own \\u00a7D.4 case \\u2014 \\\"a fresh genesis at a location whose journal remembers ordinal 4217 is a replacement wearing the old name\\\", the case StoreLost exists to refuse \\u2014 reached silently through the forward-compatibility path. With an attacker it is a downgrade attack: force the older binary, be met with TOFU. The ruling did not enumerate this corner, so it is a gap in its coverage rather than defiance of it, but the property it required is missing there. SAME MECHANISM, ARTIFACT ACCURACY: design \\u00a7B.3 promises \\\"a journal written by a later build must not make an older build refuse to compare\\\", and post-amendment the code is wrong in BOTH directions against it \\u2014 mixed skew (some v1 entries survive) yields a HeadLowerBound whose established_head() RAISES, so the older build does refuse; pure skew yields silent first contact. Report choice 10 still reads \\\"skipped and reported, never refused\\\", and the pinning test is named test_an_entry_from_a_later_build_is_skipped_and_reported_not_refused with the docstring \\\"A later build's journal must not make this one refuse to compare\\\" while its own assertion is isinstance(result.known, HeadLowerBound). The behaviour is pinned honestly; the name, docstring and choice 10 describe something the code no longer does. ARBITER CALL: whether \\u00a7B.3's forward-compat promise survives the amendment \\u2014 the conservative behaviour looks correct (a v2 entry could hold a higher ordinal, so K really is only a bound), so it is the PROMISE that should bend. Gate report: docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice3-wp1-gate-report.md on branch slice3/wp1-gate @ 14a5590d.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}
4246:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17WFF42W9782GAXN2PPRSHK","kind":"finding","ts":1788044884.980826,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-gate-all-entries-unreadable-silent-tofu\", \"status\": \"open\", \"agent\": \"arbiter\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"arbiter\", \"message\": \"ARBITER RULING (the gate asked): the conservative behavior is right, and \\u00a7B.3's forward-compat promise BENDS BY RE-SCOPING, not breaking. Ruled: (1) content-present-but-nothing-readable (skipped>0, epoch empty) is NOT first contact and NOT an EstablishedHead \\u2014 it is a third known-state carrying no ordinal; established_head() RAISES IndeterminateComparison naming the unreadable lines; with no bound, ROLLBACK is unanswerable too \\u2014 every comparison declines. First-contact TOFU requires a journal with no surviving-or-skipped content claims. (2) The promise re-scopes: individual entries from a later build are skipped and reported, never refused ON THEIR ACCOUNT \\u2014 that survives verbatim; what changes is that a comparison holding zero readable evidence DECLINES rather than answering, which refuses no entry. Resolution paths are operator work: run the newer build (which reads its own entries) or the trust-reset ceremony. Version-skew UX consequence accepted openly: an older CLI against a purely-newer journal declines comparisons \\u2014 honest, and symmetric with the mixed-skew case that already raises. (3) choice 10, the test name ..._skipped_and_reported_not_refused, and its docstring rewrite to the re-scoped promise \\u2014 the gate showed name/docstring describe what the code no longer does. This amends the arbiter-ratified \\u00a7B.3/\\u00a7choice-10 text; receipts on this fold. Routed to s3wp1-impl with BLOCKING-1.\"}"}
4248:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17WW1HT4D2PD8W1N9XRRJPF","kind":"finding","ts":1788045297.068487,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-gate-all-entries-unreadable-silent-tofu\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-impl\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"implementer\", \"message\": \"BLOCKING-2 FIXED at 1df5c8f4 (branch slice3/arrival-witness, tip fbdc5770), ruling applied as written. HeadUnreadable is the THIRD known-state: content claimed, none of it readable, NO ORDINAL CARRIED. established_head() raises IndeterminateComparison naming the unreadable lines, and because the type has no at_least field, ROLLBACK is unanswerable too by construction \\u2014 every comparison declines, exactly as ruled. First-contact TOFU now requires a journal with no surviving-or-skipped content claims; a header-only journal still qualifies (own test). The distinction in one line: an empty journal says nothing was ever accepted here, while unreadable content says heads WERE accepted and their ordinals are what was lost \\u2014 collapsing them grants trust-on-first-use PRECISELY BECAUSE the journal became unreadable. \\u00a7B.3's forward-compat promise re-scoped in code and prose as ruled: choice 10 in the report, the _parse_entry docstring, and the test renamed to test_a_later_build_entry_is_never_refused_on_its_own_account with its docstring rewritten to say what the code now does (skipped and reported, never refused ON ITS OWN ACCOUNT; a comparison holding zero readable evidence declines, which refuses no entry). Version-skew consequence stated openly in the docstring and the test: an older build against a purely newer journal declines its comparisons; resolution is operator work (newer build, or trust-reset). Mutation demo: remove the HeadUnreadable arm so known falls through to None -> 3 failed, led by test_a_journal_of_nothing_but_later_build_entries_declines. Also in the same commit, the third gate item: HeadLowerBound.__doc__ completed with the half the gate found missing \\u2014 nothing bounds the accepted head from ABOVE, so ADVANCED is as unobtainable as UNCHANGED; only the refusal below the bound survives.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}
4251:{"t":"fact","id":"01M17X8EGB91K0QY0EMVPCN1T1","kind":"finding","ts":1788045703.034126,"observer":"","origin":"","payload":"{\"name\": \"s3wp1-gate-all-entries-unreadable-silent-tofu\", \"agent\": \"s3wp1-gate\", \"slice\": \"3\", \"wp\": \"1\", \"role\": \"gate\", \"message\": \"S3WP1-GATE-2 CLOSED \\u2014 verified fixed at 1df5c8f4 (tip fbdc5770). HeadUnreadable is a third known-state for \\\"content was claimed and none of it could be read\\\", distinct from None. dataclasses.fields(HeadUnreadable) == ['skipped'] \\u2014 NO ordinal, by construction \\u2014 so unlike HeadLowerBound it cannot even answer rollback, which is correct since there is no bound to be below; it has no .entry, .at_least, .head or .ordinal. Re-ran my own pure-skew demonstration (v2 header + v2 entries, version skew, no attacker): known is now HeadUnreadable with all three losses recorded, established_head() RAISES IndeterminateComparison, and the fresh-genesis-against-a-journal-remembering-4217 case is DECLINED where it previously answered first-contact. The message states the point in words rather than leaving it to inference (\\\"nothing is known about the head it accepted \\u2014 not even a lower bound. This is NOT first contact: heads were accepted here\\\"). First contact stays reachable where it is honest: empty AND header-only journals both give known=None, no skips, and classify FIRST_CONTACT. The re-scoped \\u00a7B.3 promise landed in all three claimed places and honestly \\u2014 choice 10 (\\\"never refused ON ITS OWN ACCOUNT ... what the promise never licensed is the stronger reading\\\"), the _parse_entry docstring (which also states the version-skew consequence outright: an older build reading a purely newer journal declines, resolution is operator work), and the misleadingly-named test is GONE (zero occurrences), replaced by test_a_later_build_entry_is_never_refused_on_its_own_account. My adversarial shortcut attempt re-run against all THREE states: no attribute reaches a comparable head across all three (.entry only on EstablishedHead, .at_least only on HeadLowerBound, .head/.ordinal on none), and established_head() raises on both weakened states. Mutation demo re-run by me: removing the HeadUnreadable arm = 3 failed, 88 passed, led by test_a_journal_of_nothing_but_later_build_entries_declines; restored byte-clean. The arbiter question I routed is answered in the code the way I suggested \\u2014 the conservative behaviour kept, the PROMISE re-scoped. Gate re-check: docs/scratch/arrival-break/slice3-wp1-gate-report.md on slice3/wp1-gate @ b7e3d07d.\", \"ref\": \"design:arrival-break-slice3-witness-minimum\", \"ref_ref\": \"01M17S26ZC1JFC51VEVSG4ZA67\"}"}

